Testing code for "From Motion Blur to Motion Flow" v1.0
Project page: https://donggong1.github.io/blur2mflow

This package contains the testing code for the paper:

Dong Gong, Jie Yang, Lingqiao Liu, Yanning Zhang, Ian Reid, Chunhua Shen, Anton van den Hengel, and Qinfeng Shi. "From Motion Blur to Motion Flow: a Deep Learning Solution for Removing Heterogeneous Motion Blur". In IEEE Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 2017.

If you use this code in your research, please cite our paper:
@inproceedings{gong2017blur2fm,
  title={From Motion Blur to Motion Flow: a Deep Learning Solution for Removing Heterogeneous Motion Blur},
  author={Gong, Dong and Yang, Jie and Liu, Lingqiao and Zhang, Yanning and Reid, Ian and Shen, Chunhua and Hengel, Anton van den and Shi, Qinfeng},
  booktitle = {The IEEE Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},
  Year = {2017}
}

For algorithmic details, please refer to our paper.


=== Some hints for usage
This package contains a trained model and the code for non-blind deconvolution.

==== Motion flow estimation
Caffe is required for running this code. The folder "./model" contains the network definition in "fcn_blur2mflow.prototxt" and the trained weights in "fcn_blur2mflow.caffemodel". The code is tested on Ubuntu 14.04 with an NVIDIA GeForce 980 Ti graphics card.

To estimate motion flow map from blurred image, run:
	python script_mf_est.py [input path for testing image] [output path for motion flow map]
	e.g. python script_mf_est.py example/example1.png example/example1_mfmap.mat

Function @draw_mfmap can be used for visualizing the motion flow. Please find an example in "script_nbd.m".


==== Non-blind deconvolution (recovering sharp image given motion flow)
The implementation is in MATLAB and partly in C using mex (in folder "./mf_nbd_code"). Given a blurred image (e.g. img_name.png) and the corresponding motion flow map (e.g. img_name_mfmap.mat), the sharp image can be recovered using "nbd_single.m". An example is given in the file "script_nbd.m".

Note that the non-blind deconvolution is based on the implementation of the paper "J. Sun, W. Cao, Z. Xu, and J. Ponce. Learning a convolutional neural network for non-uniform motion blur removal. In CVPR, 2015.". The performance of the non-blind deconvolution correlates to the regularization weight. You may need to slightly tune the parameters for a desired results. 


